  "worked_solution": "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\})=3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
